  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA warm front will lift north across the area tonight and allow\nfor warm and humid conditions to return for the weekend. For\nSaturday, a mid-level shortwave will move through the Ohio\nValley and allow for shower and thunderstorm development. With a\nconditionally unstable air mass, it is possible for a storm to\nbecome stronger with gusty winds, hail, and heavy rain during\nthe peak heating hours on Saturday afternoon and evening. For\nSunday, there is growing consensus on a cold front approaching\nand slowly moving south across the forecast area. This front\nshould provide lift to generate at least some convective\nactivity on Sunday. However, the air mass should be less\nsuitable after Saturday's activity and shower/storm coverage\nshould be lower.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe forecast for Tuesday and Wednesday is trending more\nunsettled as the forecast area will be in the middle ground of a\nridge to the south and a trough moving through Canada. The ridge\nshould help keep the area dry on Monday with high pressure\nmoving through the region. However, the ridge will back off a\nbit for Tuesday and Wednesday, allowing for some of the energy\nof the upper trough to enter the Great Lakes, coincident with a\ncold front moving through the region. The air mass ahead of this\nsystem will be warm and humid and again conditionally unstable\nto allow for scattered shower and storm activity next week. Will\nneed to watch this portion of the forecast for trends as it\napproaches closer.",
